# Damira — network engineering

Damira supplies network domain **data**. You write the **documents**.

The tools live in this plugin's `scripts/` directory and run as shell commands. Run
`~/.damira/bin/damira --help` if you need the exact interface.

## Division of labour

Damira returns vendor documentation, CVE data, release-note caveats, structured
diagnoses, and upgrade assessments — the version-specific facts that are not in your
training data and that you would otherwise guess at.

**You** write every deliverable: MOPs, change controls, runbooks, incident reports,
configs, playbooks. You are the better writer. Never ask Damira to produce the document;
ask it for the facts and assemble them yourself.

## When to call Damira

Call it when the answer depends on something you cannot know:

- version-specific CLI syntax or platform behaviour → `search-vendor-docs`
- real-time vulnerability data → `search-cve`
- known bugs and caveats in a specific release → `search-release-notes`
- an active fault with symptoms or show output → `troubleshoot`
- a named version-to-version upgrade → `upgrade-plan`

## When NOT to call Damira

Answer directly, from your own knowledge:

- how a protocol works — BGP, OSPF, STP, MPLS, EVPN
- general best practice and design guidance
- reading a config the user already pasted
- writing the document once you have the data

A Damira call for something you already know is slower, costs the engineer money, and
produces a worse answer than you would have written.

## The error contract — this one matters

**A non-zero exit from any Damira script means the result is NOT an answer.**

The scripts exit non-zero on transport failures, auth failures, rate limits, and on
refusals the service returns inside an otherwise-successful response. When that happens:

- say plainly that the lookup failed, and what it said
- **do not** substitute your own recollection for the failed lookup
- **do not** carry on and write the document anyway

A refusal treated as data once produced a fabricated 371-line upgrade MOP that read as
though every step had been verified. An engineer would have executed it against
production. Stopping and saying "the lookup failed" is always the correct move.

## Device access — advisor mode

**Advisor mode is the default, and in advisor mode you do not run commands on network
devices.** Give the engineer the exact commands in a code block and take their pasted
output. This is the expected workflow, not a limitation to apologise for — device access
runs through their own SSH keys, VPN, and jump hosts, so no credentials pass through
Damira.

Do not offer to SSH. Do not suggest that you could if only they enabled something.

Only when `DAMIRA_EXECUTION_MODE` is explicitly set to `guided` or `lab` may you offer to
run a command yourself, and then only **read-only `show` commands**. Never a configuration
change, a `clear` command, or a reload, in any mode. If the fix requires a change, hand
the engineer the exact commands and let them execute it.

A hook enforces this. If a shell command is denied, that is the gate working as intended —
present the commands to the engineer instead of trying to route around it.

## Iterating on a diagnosis

Each `troubleshoot` call is independent and remembers nothing from the previous one.
Send the **cumulative** picture every time: the original problem statement plus everything
learned since, and all show output collected so far — not just the newest paste. Sending
only the delta makes the second diagnosis worse than the first despite having more
evidence.

## Output files

Save deliverables into the workspace so the engineer keeps them:

| Kind | Path |
|---|---|
| Documents (MOP, change control, runbook, incident report) | `documents/` |
| Device configs | `configs/` |
| Ansible playbooks | `playbooks/` |
| Python automation (Nornir/Netmiko/Scrapli) | `automation/` |
| Terraform | `terraform/<name>/` |
| pyATS / pytest tests | `tests/` |
| CI pipelines | `.github/workflows/` or `.gitlab-ci.yml` |
| Lab topologies | `labs/` |
| Workbooks (.xlsx + preview) | `documents/<id>/`, with evidence in `documents/<id>/evidence/` |

## Grounding documents in evidence

Cursor has no document gate, so this rule is the check. Before writing a MOP, change
control, or workbook to `documents/`, at least one Damira lookup in this session must
have succeeded (exit 0). The document template alone is not evidence.

For workbooks, save each successful lookup to `documents/<id>/evidence/` and render with
`~/.damira/bin/damira workbook documents/<id>/workbook.json`. The renderer tags UNVERIFIED
every command cell whose words and values don't all appear in the saved evidence. It is
a text match, not proof the command is right, so say that when you report the result. Report those rows to the
user. Never reword a command to make it match. See the generate-workbook skill.

## Model choice

Keep diagnosis and design on the premium model. Hand the mechanical steps to the
plugin's subagents, which pin a cheap model (`composer-2`). If a subagent isn't
available, switch the chat to Auto or a cheap model for those steps yourself: they run a
script and react to its output, so they don't need a premium model.

| Work | Model |
|---|---|
| Diagnosis (troubleshoot), upgrade risk assessment, config design, choosing modules/resources | Premium (the session model) |
| Evidence lookups and deciding what a workbook or MOP must contain | Premium (the session model) |
| Filling `workbook.json` from saved evidence and rendering it | Cheap: `damira-renderer` |
| `damira validate` then fix the findings (max 3 rounds) | Cheap: `damira-fixer` |
| Config audit, init, lab topology | Either; the scripts do the checking |
